# FinnOS base image — x86_64, the release target.
#
# One package per line, as mkarchiso reads it: no variables, no shell, and
# a comment only at the start of a line. base/setup.sh reads the same file,
# so what the disc carries and what a machine installs cannot drift.
#
# The machine this profile is built and checked against is a Dell OptiPlex
# 3020: an i3-4130 (Haswell), Intel HD Graphics 4400, 4 GB of RAM and a
# 1080p panel. It is a general Arch x86_64 profile, not a model-specific
# one: what is below is what Arch's own hardware support expects on an
# Intel or AMD machine, and a package that only made sense on that one
# desktop would not belong here.
#
# What is deliberately absent is as much of the list as what is present:
# no X server, no Wayland compositor, no GTK, no Qt, no desktop environment.
# base/pacman.conf refuses them by name.

# The init system and the shell the disc boots into.
base
systemd
systemd-sysvcompat
sudo
util-linux
kbd

# The kernel, its firmware and the DRM/KMS stack the compositor drives.
# linux-firmware is the GPU firmware for Intel, AMD and NVIDIA parts, the
# Wi-Fi and Bluetooth firmware, and the firmware the i915 driver loads on
# newer Intel chips. Both microcode packages are here because the profile
# is a general one and the bootloader loads whichever the CPU needs; on the
# OptiPlex that is intel-ucode. A machine without linux-firmware has no
# Wi-Fi and no Bluetooth, which is the failure this list exists to avoid.
linux
linux-firmware
intel-ucode
amd-ucode
libdrm
libinput
seatd

# Mesa provides the GBM and EGL the accelerated path uses; libglvnd is the
# EGL/GLES dispatch, which is what pkg-config's egl and glesv2 look for.
# vulkan-intel is ANV, the Vulkan driver for Intel GPUs from Haswell on,
# which is the OptiPlex's HD 4400 and every Intel desktop since. Mesa's own
# dependencies name `wayland`, the client library; it is not what
# pacman.conf refuses (that is the compositors), and this is why.
mesa
libglvnd
vulkan-intel
wayland
wayland-protocols
libxkbcommon

# Wine runs 32-bit programs on x86_64 even inside a 64-bit prefix, so its
# GL and Vulkan need the multilib copies too. base/pacman.multilib.conf is
# what puts [multilib] in front of pacman; without it this list does not
# resolve, which is a build error rather than a machine without Wine.
lib32-mesa
lib32-vulkan-icd-loader
lib32-vulkan-intel

# What finn-comp links against.
freetype2
fontconfig
libwebp
alsa-lib
alsa-ucm-conf

# Audio. PipeWire is the session's sound server, pipewire-pulse is the
# PulseAudio socket a Wine program and a Flatpak app expect to find, and
# wireplumber is the session manager that gives it devices. The compositor
# is not a sound server and does not pretend to be one.
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
wireplumber

# Bluetooth. bluez is the daemon and bluez-utils is bluetoothctl. The
# OptiPlex has no radio of its own; a USB dongle is what makes these do
# anything, and the service's own condition keeps it from starting on a
# machine with no controller.
bluez
bluez-utils

# A font to fall back to when Fixel Display is missing. The shell ships its
# own face; this is what a text editor on the machine can use.
ttf-dejavu

# NetworkManager configures connections; wpa_supplicant provides Wi-Fi.
networkmanager
wpa_supplicant
openssh
curl

# Flatpak package management and Wine compatibility target the x86_64
# release image. Flatpak GUI apps require Wayland surfaces hosted by
# finn-comp. wine-mono and wine-gecko are the two runtimes Wine downloads
# on first run on a machine with a network; carrying them means a machine
# without one still runs a .NET or Gecko-based installer. winetricks is
# the verb list the Windows page of the Store drives.
flatpak
wine
winetricks
wine-mono
wine-gecko

# jq and zstd: zstd is what pacman
# writes and the live medium's squashfs is compressed with. Both are on the
# disc so an installed machine has them without a network.
jq
zstd

# The installer and the tools it needs: pacstrap is arch-install-scripts,
# and a disk is partitioned and formatted before it is installed on.
arch-install-scripts
dosfstools
e2fsprogs
parted
gptfdisk

# The bootloader the installed machine gets. GRUB is installed on the
# machine by the installer, from the machine's own package set; it is on
# the medium too, so that a machine can also be installed by hand.
grub
efibootmgr
# syslinux, mtools, squashfs-tools and xorriso are not here: they are what
# the machine that writes the disc needs, not what the disc needs. See
# "Building the medium" in README.md.

# Python for FinnSDK and the shell's tooling; git and the compiler because
# the machine the disc is installed on builds the compositor from the
# sources the disc carries.
python
git
nano
vim
base-devel
pkgconf
# The local packages in base/pkgbuilds are deliberately not in this list:
# nothing on the disc installs from a repository that does not exist. The
# compositor and the SDK are built by the machine, from the sources.
